  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Streaming Data**\n\nI\'ve successfully gathered the necessary data on `https://nxsha.space`. I\'ve confirmed that the API endpoints like `/api/home-feed?tabKey=home`, along with `movies`, `tv-shows`, and `anime`, supply live streaming catalog data. I\'m focusing on parsing and understanding the structure of this data now.\n\n\n**Defining Data Structure**\n\nI\'m now focusing on defining the data structures gleaned from the streaming data. Specifically, I\'m noting the presence of `sliderItems`, `fetchedSections`, and `discoveryCatalogs`, each containing titles, IDs, media types, dates, and various metadata like ratings, descriptions, and related images. Also, I\'ve noted the watch page URLs for movies and TV shows to plan implementation. My thoughts are shifting toward how we will create local fallbacks to ensure consistency.\n\n\n**Planning Feature Verification**\n\nI\'m developing a verification plan for the catalog browsing, filtering, and searching features of the streaming platform. My focus is on user journeys: how a user interacts with the system, starting from the landing page and proceeding through search and filtering. I will test both the Nxsha source feed and multi-platform catalog features and their corresponding metadata. The goal is to ensure functionality across different scenarios and filter options.\n\n\n**Implementing Interactive Features**\n\nMy current focus is on implementing interactive features like adaptive bitrate playback, offline downloads, and social sharing. I\'m visualizing how the video player modal will function, including the bitrate controls and the Nxsha source selector. I\'m also considering the \"Download Offline\" feature, which downloads items into local storage, as well as the social sharing options, which enable users to share timestamped clips and encrypted watch links. I\'m also planning the Watch Party and Couple Watch Party features with real-time WebSocket sync.\n\n\n**Designing Interactive Features**\n\nI am now thinking about synchronized playback controls, couple pulse triggers (`Send Heartbeat`, `Sync Timestamp`), and live room chat, all persisted on the server. I have been envisioning an AI concierge with deep analysis powered by Gemini for lore/thematic analysis, recommendations, scene recaps, and real-time voice conversation. I\'ve also been considering the \"Discover\" home screen with platform source filters, personalized recommendations, and a trending catalog. Finally, I\'ve outlined the \"Watch Party & Couple Lounge\" view, and the \"Offline Vault\" features.\n\n\n**Expanding Feature Suite**\n\nI\'m now integrating offline network simulation, download bitrate selection, and a quota meter. My efforts are also directed toward building an AI Studio with Gemini-powered tools, including personalized recommendations, lore explainers, trivia, and real-time voice interaction via WebSockets. I\'m also planning a Security & Privacy Vault with watch history encryption, incognito mode, DRM controls, and notification preferences. The frontend design is evolving, with a top bar constitution enforcing a clean, streamlined user interface with clear navigation.\n\n\n**Refining User Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the user interface elements and their placement. The \"Start Watch Party\" button will be the primary action, and secondary tools will reside in the Workspace Toolbar, right below the header. I\'m also planning to use clean, unboxed text with separators for metadata. I\'m selecting specific fonts: Outfit, Plus Jakarta Sans, and JetBrains Mono for a clean visual. I\'m focusing on the MediaPoster to handle image loading failures, fallback to a deterministic SVG cinema artwork canvas.\n\n\n**Enhancing Image Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on robust image handling with the `<MediaPoster>` component. My idea is to render deterministic SVG cinema art posters by default. Then, if the TMDB image loads cleanly, it layers over the SVG. I want to test this out now, or alternatively proxy or generate data-URI SVG posters on either the server or client side to ensure every `<img>` tag loads with a 200 OK status and avoids triggering failed network requests.\n\n\n**Developing Stream Engine Logic**\n\nI\'m now designing `src/utils/streamEngine.ts`, focusing on core logic. I\'m prioritizing `calculateAdaptiveBitrate`, defining bitrate and codec profiles based on bandwidth and user settings. Then, I\'ll tackle `computePersonalizedRecommendations`, weighing genre affinity, watch history, and likes to rank media for the user. I\'ll write comprehensive unit tests too.\n\n\n**Defining Core Utility Functions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on core utility functions. I\'m developing a function to create a human-readable list of match reasons for recommendations. I\'m also working on `evaluateWatchPartySync`, `filterAndSearchCatalog`, `formatStorageQuota`, and `obfuscatePrivacyToken` for the security vault. I\'ll focus on unit tests next.\n\n\n"
