      text: "# PROJECT: AETHO-STYLE SOCIAL WATCH PARTY PLATFORM\n\nAct as a senior full-stack developer, UI/UX designer, and real-time systems architect. Build a complete, responsive, production-oriented movie and TV watch-party web application inspired by the visual experience of https://aethoflix.world/ and the media discovery/embed concept of https://nxsha.space/.\n\nCreate an original product identity. Do not copy their logos, proprietary assets, or exact page designs.\n\nThe application must be a working web app, not just a static landing page or UI mockup.\n\n## 1. PRODUCT VISION\n\nBuild a cinematic platform where users can discover movies and shows, create private watch rooms, and watch together remotely.\n\nThe product has two primary experiences:\n\n1. Couple Mode \342\200\224 a romantic, private, personalized cinema for two people.\n2. Group Mode \342\200\224 a social cinema for friends, classmates, and communities.\n\nSuggested product name: CineTogether.\n\nTagline: \"Different places. One screen. Shared moments.\"\n\nUse the name as a configurable brand constant so it can be changed later.\n\n## 2. DESIGN AND USER EXPERIENCE\n\nCreate a premium, modern streaming interface with:\n\n- Deep charcoal and near-black backgrounds.\n- Cinematic posters and large landscape backdrops.\n- Purple, violet, and electric-blue accent gradients.\n- Subtle glassmorphism for floating controls.\n- Rounded cards, soft shadows, smooth transitions, and elegant hover states.\n- Beautiful empty states, loading skeletons, toast notifications, and error recovery.\n- Responsive layouts for desktop, laptop, tablet, and mobile.\n- Accessible contrast, keyboard navigation, and reduced-motion support.\n\nUse a streaming-service layout with a persistent navigation bar:\n\nHome | Movies | TV Shows | Anime | My List | Watch Parties\n\nAdd a prominent \"Create Watch Party\" action.\n\nThe home page should include:\n- Large cinematic hero banner.\n- Trending movies and shows.\n- Continue Watching.\n- Popular this week.\n- Recommended for you.\n- Genres and languages.\n- Recently added.\n- Couple Night recommendations.\n- Group Movie Night recommendations.\n- Search with instant results.\n\nUse real metadata and posters only through permitted APIs, such as TMDB with the required API configuration and attribution. If live API credentials are unavailable, provide realistic demo data and a clear demo-data indicator.\n\nNever invent working streaming links or claim that demo playback is live.\n\n## 3. TWO DISTINCT WATCH-PARTY MODES\n\nWhen a user selects \"Create Watch Party\", display two beautiful selectable cards.\n\n### MODE A: COUPLE CINEMA \342\235\244\357\270\217\n\nCreate a dedicated experience for exactly two participants.\n\nVisual direction:\n- Romantic purple, rose, pink, and midnight-blue gradients.\n- Soft ambient lighting and subtle animated particles.\n- Intimate cinema-room layout.\n- Optional heart animations, romantic room themes, and shared watch history.\n- Elegant, minimal controls that keep attention on the movie.\n\nFeatures:\n- Create a private room.\n- Generate a unique invite link and room code.\n- Invite a partner through the native share dialog or copy-link action.\n- Optional room password.\n- Two participant slots with online/offline presence.\n- Shared movie selection.\n- Synchronized playback where the media provider supports programmatic control.\n- Real-time text chat.\n- Emoji reactions and floating hearts.\n- Quick reactions such as \342\235\244\357\270\217 \360\237\230\202 \360\237\230\256 \360\237\230\242 \360\237\224\245.\n- Shared watchlist.\n- \"Next movie?\" voting.\n- Optional date-night themes and room backgrounds.\n- Leave-room and end-session controls.\n\nThe interface should feel personal and immersive, not like a corporate meeting tool.\n\nRoom states:\n- Waiting for partner.\n- Partner joined.\n- Selecting a movie.\n- Ready to watch.\n- Playing.\n- Paused.\n- Reconnecting.\n- Session ended.\n\nDo not make either partner\'s camera or microphone mandatory.\n\n### MODE B: FRIENDS & GROUP CINEMA \360\237\216\211\n\nCreate a separate experience for 3\342\200\22320 participants by default, with a configurable room capacity.\n\nVisual direction:\n- Dark cinematic interface with blue/violet lighting.\n- Large shared player.\n- Participant avatars.\n- Expandable chat sidebar.\n- Optional grid of participant tiles when video chat is enabled.\n\nFeatures:\n- Create a group room.\n- Unique invite links and room codes.\n- Join an existing room.\n- Room title, description, and optional password.\n- Host and moderator roles.\n- Participant list with presence indicators.\n- Real-time group chat.\n- Emoji reactions.\n- Host-controlled play and pause when supported.\n- Synchronized playback and seeking when supported.\n- Host transfer.\n- Mute chat notifications.\n- Vote for the next movie.\n- Group watchlist.\n- Room capacity and join/leave notifications.\n- Optional WebRTC voice/video chat with explicit permission.\n- Optional push-to-talk or mute controls.\n- Host controls for removing disruptive participants and locking the room.\n\nOnly the host or authorized moderators should control shared playback by default. Allow the host to enable collaborative controls in room settings.\n\nOn mobile, use a compact bottom control bar and a collapsible chat panel.\n\n## 4. MEDIA CATALOG AND PLAYER INTEGRATION\n\nUse https://nxsha.space/ as the requested reference for movie/show discovery and its media embed concept.\n\nInvestigate the documented integration surface at:\nhttps://web.nxsha.space/embed\n\nUse this documented route pattern as an integration reference:\n- Movie: /embed/movie/{mediaId}\n- TV episode: /embed/tv/{mediaId}/{season}/{episode}\n\nTreat these as candidate integration routes, not a guarantee that every title or provider is playable.\n\nImplement a dedicated MediaProviderAdapter interface with:\n- getEmbedUrl()\n- initializePlayer()\n- play()\n- pause()\n- seekTo()\n- getCurrentTime()\n- getDuration()\n- onPlaybackEvent()\n- destroy()\n\nImplement only the methods supported by the actual provider and its documented interface. Detect unsupported operations rather than pretending they work.\n\nImportant integration rules:\n- Do not scrape protected pages or bypass DRM, access controls, paywalls, or provider restrictions.\n- Use only media that the user is authorized to access and embed.\n- Do not assume a third-party iframe can be controlled by the parent application.\n- Cross-origin browser security may prevent access to iframe playback state.\n- If the provider offers no supported playback-control API, show the video player normally and provide a clearly labelled \"Sync unavailable for this player\" state.\n- Never show a fake synchronized playback status.\n- Add a configurable provider system so authorized sources can be added later.\n- If Nxsha is unavailable, use a licensed sample video or locally hosted demo media for testing.\n- Do not silently replace a failed stream with a different movie.\n\nProvide:\n- Movie poster and backdrop.\n- Title, year, overview, rating, and genres.\n- Movie/show selection.\n- Season and episode selectors for TV shows.\n- Player loading and failure states.\n- Retry and supported-server selection, if the provider exposes these capabilities.\n- Fullscreen support where permitted.\n- Subtitle and language selection only where supported.\n- A clear external-provider notice.\n\n## 5. REAL-TIME PLAYBACK SYNCHRONIZATION\n\nBuild synchronization as a real system, not just matching play buttons.\n\nFor compatible players, use WebSocket-based room events and an authoritative host playback state.\n\nExample room state:\n{\n  \"roomId\": \"room_123\",\n  \"mediaId\": \"movie_550\",\n  \"mediaType\": \"movie\",\n  \"status\": \"playing\",\n  \"positionSeconds\": 120,\n  \"updatedAt\": \"server_timestamp\",\n  \"hostId\": \"user_123\",\n  \"revision\": 1\n}\n\nImplement:\n- Host play/pause events.\n- Seek events.\n- Periodic playback-position updates.\n- Join-in-progress synchronization.\n- Reconnection and state recovery.\n- Event ordering and stale-event rejection.\n- Small drift correction when supported.\n- Larger-drift resynchronization when supported.\n- Server-authoritative timestamps.\n- Graceful handling of network delays.\n\nNever allow repeated synchronization events to create an infinite feedback loop.\n\nWhen the player does not expose playback state or controls, do not claim frame-accurate synchronization. Offer an honest fallback that can coordinate room entry and media selection, with manual playback instructions.\n\n## 6. AUTHENTICATION AND USER PROFILES\n\nProvide:\n- Guest mode for trying the demo.\n- Email/password registration and login.\n- Optional Google sign-in if configured.\n- User profile and avatar.\n- Recently watched titles.\n- Personal watchlist.\n- Created and joined rooms.\n- Session history and preferences.\n\nUse Supabase Auth or Firebase Authentication. Choose one backend and implement it consistently.\n\nNever place secret API keys in client-side source code.\n\n## 7. TECHNICAL STACK\n\nPreferred stack:\n- React.\n- TypeScript.\n- Vite.\n- Tailwind CSS.\n- React Router.\n- Lucide icons.\n- Framer Motion for subtle transitions.\n- Supabase for authentication, database, and realtime functionality, or a consistently implemented Firebase alternative.\n- WebSockets for real-time playback events if the selected backend requires a separate socket service.\n- WebRTC for optional participant audio/video.\n\nUse modular components and a clean folder structure.\n\nSuggested components:\n- AppShell\n- NavigationBar\n- HeroBanner\n- MediaCard\n- MediaCarousel\n- SearchOverlay\n- WatchPartyModeSelector\n- CoupleRoom\n- GroupRoom\n- MediaPlayer\n- PlayerControls\n- ParticipantList\n- ChatPanel\n- ReactionLayer\n- InviteDialog\n- RoomSettings\n- Watchlist\n- ToastProvider\n\n## 8. DATABASE DESIGN\n\nCreate the necessary migrations or schema definitions.\n\nTables:\n- profiles\n- media_items\n- watchlists\n- watchlist_items\n- watch_rooms\n- room_members\n- playback_states\n- chat_messages\n- room_reactions\n- room_votes\n- watch_sessions\n\nUse UUID primary keys, timestamps, appropriate indexes, and foreign keys.\n\nRoom types:\n- couple\n- group\n\nRoom fields should include:\n- id\n- room_code\n- room_type\n- host_user_id\n- current_media_id\n- is_private\n- password_hash, if password protection is implemented\n- max_participants\n- created_at\n- expires_at\n- status\n\nDo not store plaintext room passwords.\n\nConfigure row-level security policies. A private room\'s chat, playback state, and member list must not be readable by unauthorized users.\n\nValidate room membership on the server. Do not trust a user ID or host role supplied by the browser.\n\n## 9. REQUIRED PAGES\n\nBuild all of the following:\n\n1. Home and media discovery.\n2. Movies.\n3. TV Shows.\n4. Anime.\n5. Search results.\n6. Movie/show detail page.\n7. Create Watch Party.\n8. Join Watch Party.\n9. Couple Cinema room.\n10. Group Cinema room.\n11. My Watchlist.\n12. Profile and preferences.\n13. Authentication.\n14. Not found and error pages.\n\nInclude proper routes and working navigation.\n\n## 10. FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\nEvery visible interactive element must have a working action.\n\nImplement:\n- Search and filtering.\n- Genre and language selection.\n- Media detail navigation.\n- Create room.\n- Join room by code.\n- Copy invite link.\n- Share invite link.\n- Leave room.\n- Update participant presence.\n- Send and receive chat messages.\n- Send emoji reactions.\n- Vote for a movie.\n- Add/remove watchlist items.\n- Change room settings.\n- Playback controls when supported.\n- Responsive mobile navigation.\n- Loading, success, empty, and error states.\n\nPersist the appropriate data in the configured backend.\n\nUse demo mode when backend credentials are missing. Clearly distinguish demo functionality from production functionality. Do not simulate successful database writes without explaining that demo mode is active.\n\n## 11. SECURITY AND RELIABILITY\n\n- Validate all inputs.\n- Rate-limit room creation and chat where supported.\n- Prevent unauthorized room access.\n- Sanitize user-generated content.\n- Protect against unauthorized host actions.\n- Do not expose secrets in browser code.\n- Handle API failures and network disconnections.\n- Clean up WebSocket and WebRTC connections on exit.\n- Avoid autoplay with sound before user interaction.\n- Use only permitted media sources and respect provider restrictions.\n\n## 12. BUILD AND DELIVERY REQUIREMENTS\n\nDo not stop after generating a homepage.\n\nFirst create the application structure and implement the essential user flows. Then test and fix the app.\n\nBuild in this order:\n1. App shell and design system.\n2. Media catalog and search.\n3. Authentication and room database.\n4. Couple mode.\n5. Group mode.\n6. Chat and presence.\n7. Playback adapter and synchronization.\n8. Responsive polish.\n9. Error handling and testing.\n\nAcceptance tests:\n- A user can browse demo movie data.\n- A user can create a couple room.\n- A second user can join using the invite link.\n- A user can create a group room.\n- Participants can send messages and see new messages in real time.\n- Room access restrictions are enforced.\n- Watchlist changes persist.\n- Supported players synchronize correctly.\n- Unsupported players show an honest limitation.\n- The website works on mobile and desktop.\n- No major navigation button is inert.\n- The project builds without TypeScript errors.\n\nAt completion, provide:\n- The implemented application.\n- Project folder structure.\n- Required environment variables in .env.example.\n- Database schema and migrations.\n- Setup and run instructions.\n- Deployment instructions for Vercel and the selected backend.\n- A checklist of working features and integrations that still require credentials or provider approval.\n\nStart building the actual application now. Prioritize functional features, robust architecture, and a polished cinematic user experience over placeholder screens."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# PROJECT: AETHO-STYLE SOCIAL WATCH PARTY PLATFORM\n\nAct as a senior full-stack developer, UI/UX designer, and real-time systems architect. Build a complete, responsive, production-oriented movie and TV watch-party web application inspired by the visual experience of https://aethoflix.world/ and the media discovery/embed concept of https://nxsha.space/.\n\nCreate an original product identity. Do not copy their logos, proprietary assets, or exact page designs.\n\nThe application must be a working web app, not just a static landing page or UI mockup.\n\n## 1. PRODUCT VISION\n\nBuild a cinematic platform where users can discover movies and shows, create private watch rooms, and watch together remotely.\n\nThe product has two primary experiences:\n\n1. Couple Mode \342\200\224 a romantic, private, personalized cinema for two people.\n2. Group Mode \342\200\224 a social cinema for friends, classmates, and communities.\n\nSuggested product name: CineTogether.\n\nTagline: \"Different places. One screen. Shared moments.\"\n\nUse the name as a configurable brand constant so it can be changed later.\n\n## 2. DESIGN AND USER EXPERIENCE\n\nCreate a premium, modern streaming interface with:\n\n- Deep charcoal and near-black backgrounds.\n- Cinematic posters and large landscape backdrops.\n- Purple, violet, and electric-blue accent gradients.\n- Subtle glassmorphism for floating controls.\n- Rounded cards, soft shadows, smooth transitions, and elegant hover states.\n- Beautiful empty states, loading skeletons, toast notifications, and error recovery.\n- Responsive layouts for desktop, laptop, tablet, and mobile.\n- Accessible contrast, keyboard navigation, and reduced-motion support.\n\nUse a streaming-service layout with a persistent navigation bar:\n\nHome | Movies | TV Shows | Anime | My List | Watch Parties\n\nAdd a prominent \"Create Watch Party\" action.\n\nThe home page should include:\n- Large cinematic hero banner.\n- Trending movies and shows.\n- Continue Watching.\n- Popular this week.\n- Recommended for you.\n- Genres and languages.\n- Recently added.\n- Couple Night recommendations.\n- Group Movie Night recommendations.\n- Search with instant results.\n\nUse real metadata and posters only through permitted APIs, such as TMDB with the required API configuration and attribution. If live API credentials are unavailable, provide realistic demo data and a clear demo-data indicator.\n\nNever invent working streaming links or claim that demo playback is live.\n\n## 3. TWO DISTINCT WATCH-PARTY MODES\n\nWhen a user selects \"Create Watch Party\", display two beautiful selectable cards.\n\n### MODE A: COUPLE CINEMA \342\235\244\357\270\217\n\nCreate a dedicated experience for exactly two participants.\n\nVisual direction:\n- Romantic purple, rose, pink, and midnight-blue gradients.\n- Soft ambient lighting and subtle animated particles.\n- Intimate cinema-room layout.\n- Optional heart animations, romantic room themes, and shared watch history.\n- Elegant, minimal controls that keep attention on the movie.\n\nFeatures:\n- Create a private room.\n- Generate a unique invite link and room code.\n- Invite a partner through the native share dialog or copy-link action.\n- Optional room password.\n- Two participant slots with online/offline presence.\n- Shared movie selection.\n- Synchronized playback where the media provider supports programmatic control.\n- Real-time text chat.\n- Emoji reactions and floating hearts.\n- Quick reactions such as \342\235\244\357\270\217 \360\237\230\202 \360\237\230\256 \360\237\230\242 \360\237\224\245.\n- Shared watchlist.\n- \"Next movie?\" voting.\n- Optional date-night themes and room backgrounds.\n- Leave-room and end-session controls.\n\nThe interface should feel personal and immersive, not like a corporate meeting tool.\n\nRoom states:\n- Waiting for partner.\n- Partner joined.\n- Selecting a movie.\n- Ready to watch.\n- Playing.\n- Paused.\n- Reconnecting.\n- Session ended.\n\nDo not make either partner\'s camera or microphone mandatory.\n\n### MODE B: FRIENDS & GROUP CINEMA \360\237\216\211\n\nCreate a separate experience for 3\342\200\22320 participants by default, with a configurable room capacity.\n\nVisual direction:\n- Dark cinematic interface with blue/violet lighting.\n- Large shared player.\n- Participant avatars.\n- Expandable chat sidebar.\n- Optional grid of participant tiles when video chat is enabled.\n\nFeatures:\n- Create a group room.\n- Unique invite links and room codes.\n- Join an existing room.\n- Room title, description, and optional password.\n- Host and moderator roles.\n- Participant list with presence indicators.\n- Real-time group chat.\n- Emoji reactions.\n- Host-controlled play and pause when supported.\n- Synchronized playback and seeking when supported.\n- Host transfer.\n- Mute chat notifications.\n- Vote for the next movie.\n- Group watchlist.\n- Room capacity and join/leave notifications.\n- Optional WebRTC voice/video chat with explicit permission.\n- Optional push-to-talk or mute controls.\n- Host controls for removing disruptive participants and locking the room.\n\nOnly the host or authorized moderators should control shared playback by default. Allow the host to enable collaborative controls in room settings.\n\nOn mobile, use a compact bottom control bar and a collapsible chat panel.\n\n## 4. MEDIA CATALOG AND PLAYER INTEGRATION\n\nUse https://nxsha.space/ as the requested reference for movie/show discovery and its media embed concept.\n\nInvestigate the documented integration surface at:\nhttps://web.nxsha.space/embed\n\nUse this documented route pattern as an integration reference:\n- Movie: /embed/movie/{mediaId}\n- TV episode: /embed/tv/{mediaId}/{season}/{episode}\n\nTreat these as candidate integration routes, not a guarantee that every title or provider is playable.\n\nImplement a dedicated MediaProviderAdapter interface with:\n- getEmbedUrl()\n- initializePlayer()\n- play()\n- pause()\n- seekTo()\n- getCurrentTime()\n- getDuration()\n- onPlaybackEvent()\n- destroy()\n\nImplement only the methods supported by the actual provider and its documented interface. Detect unsupported operations rather than pretending they work.\n\nImportant integration rules:\n- Do not scrape protected pages or bypass DRM, access controls, paywalls, or provider restrictions.\n- Use only media that the user is authorized to access and embed.\n- Do not assume a third-party iframe can be controlled by the parent application.\n- Cross-origin browser security may prevent access to iframe playback state.\n- If the provider offers no supported playback-control API, show the video player normally and provide a clearly labelled \"Sync unavailable for this player\" state.\n- Never show a fake synchronized playback status.\n- Add a configurable provider system so authorized sources can be added later.\n- If Nxsha is unavailable, use a licensed sample video or locally hosted demo media for testing.\n- Do not silently replace a failed stream with a different movie.\n\nProvide:\n- Movie poster and backdrop.\n- Title, year, overview, rating, and genres.\n- Movie/show selection.\n- Season and episode selectors for TV shows.\n- Player loading and failure states.\n- Retry and supported-server selection, if the provider exposes these capabilities.\n- Fullscreen support where permitted.\n- Subtitle and language selection only where supported.\n- A clear external-provider notice.\n\n## 5. REAL-TIME PLAYBACK SYNCHRONIZATION\n\nBuild synchronization as a real system, not just matching play buttons.\n\nFor compatible players, use WebSocket-based room events and an authoritative host playback state.\n\nExample room state:\n{\n  \"roomId\": \"room_123\",\n  \"mediaId\": \"movie_550\",\n  \"mediaType\": \"movie\",\n  \"status\": \"playing\",\n  \"positionSeconds\": 120,\n  \"updatedAt\": \"server_timestamp\",\n  \"hostId\": \"user_123\",\n  \"revision\": 1\n}\n\nImplement:\n- Host play/pause events.\n- Seek events.\n- Periodic playback-position updates.\n- Join-in-progress synchronization.\n- Reconnection and state recovery.\n- Event ordering and stale-event rejection.\n- Small drift correction when supported.\n- Larger-drift resynchronization when supported.\n- Server-authoritative timestamps.\n- Graceful handling of network delays.\n\nNever allow repeated synchronization events to create an infinite feedback loop.\n\nWhen the player does not expose playback state or controls, do not claim frame-accurate synchronization. Offer an honest fallback that can coordinate room entry and media selection, with manual playback instructions.\n\n## 6. AUTHENTICATION AND USER PROFILES\n\nProvide:\n- Guest mode for trying the demo.\n- Email/password registration and login.\n- Optional Google sign-in if configured.\n- User profile and avatar.\n- Recently watched titles.\n- Personal watchlist.\n- Created and joined rooms.\n- Session history and preferences.\n\nUse Supabase Auth or Firebase Authentication. Choose one backend and implement it consistently.\n\nNever place secret API keys in client-side source code.\n\n## 7. TECHNICAL STACK\n\nPreferred stack:\n- React.\n- TypeScript.\n- Vite.\n- Tailwind CSS.\n- React Router.\n- Lucide icons.\n- Framer Motion for subtle transitions.\n- Supabase for authentication, database, and realtime functionality, or a consistently implemented Firebase alternative.\n- WebSockets for real-time playback events if the selected backend requires a separate socket service.\n- WebRTC for optional participant audio/video.\n\nUse modular components and a clean folder structure.\n\nSuggested components:\n- AppShell\n- NavigationBar\n- HeroBanner\n- MediaCard\n- MediaCarousel\n- SearchOverlay\n- WatchPartyModeSelector\n- CoupleRoom\n- GroupRoom\n- MediaPlayer\n- PlayerControls\n- ParticipantList\n- ChatPanel\n- ReactionLayer\n- InviteDialog\n- RoomSettings\n- Watchlist\n- ToastProvider\n\n## 8. DATABASE DESIGN\n\nCreate the necessary migrations or schema definitions.\n\nTables:\n- profiles\n- media_items\n- watchlists\n- watchlist_items\n- watch_rooms\n- room_members\n- playback_states\n- chat_messages\n- room_reactions\n- room_votes\n- watch_sessions\n\nUse UUID primary keys, timestamps, appropriate indexes, and foreign keys.\n\nRoom types:\n- couple\n- group\n\nRoom fields should include:\n- id\n- room_code\n- room_type\n- host_user_id\n- current_media_id\n- is_private\n- password_hash, if password protection is implemented\n- max_participants\n- created_at\n- expires_at\n- status\n\nDo not store plaintext room passwords.\n\nConfigure row-level security policies. A private room\'s chat, playback state, and member list must not be readable by unauthorized users.\n\nValidate room membership on the server. Do not trust a user ID or host role supplied by the browser.\n\n## 9. REQUIRED PAGES\n\nBuild all of the following:\n\n1. Home and media discovery.\n2. Movies.\n3. TV Shows.\n4. Anime.\n5. Search results.\n6. Movie/show detail page.\n7. Create Watch Party.\n8. Join Watch Party.\n9. Couple Cinema room.\n10. Group Cinema room.\n11. My Watchlist.\n12. Profile and preferences.\n13. Authentication.\n14. Not found and error pages.\n\nInclude proper routes and working navigation.\n\n## 10. FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\nEvery visible interactive element must have a working action.\n\nImplement:\n- Search and filtering.\n- Genre and language selection.\n- Media detail navigation.\n- Create room.\n- Join room by code.\n- Copy invite link.\n- Share invite link.\n- Leave room.\n- Update participant presence.\n- Send and receive chat messages.\n- Send emoji reactions.\n- Vote for a movie.\n- Add/remove watchlist items.\n- Change room settings.\n- Playback controls when supported.\n- Responsive mobile navigation.\n- Loading, success, empty, and error states.\n\nPersist the appropriate data in the configured backend.\n\nUse demo mode when backend credentials are missing. Clearly distinguish demo functionality from production functionality. Do not simulate successful database writes without explaining that demo mode is active.\n\n## 11. SECURITY AND RELIABILITY\n\n- Validate all inputs.\n- Rate-limit room creation and chat where supported.\n- Prevent unauthorized room access.\n- Sanitize user-generated content.\n- Protect against unauthorized host actions.\n- Do not expose secrets in browser code.\n- Handle API failures and network disconnections.\n- Clean up WebSocket and WebRTC connections on exit.\n- Avoid autoplay with sound before user interaction.\n- Use only permitted media sources and respect provider restrictions.\n\n## 12. BUILD AND DELIVERY REQUIREMENTS\n\nDo not stop after generating a homepage.\n\nFirst create the application structure and implement the essential user flows. Then test and fix the app.\n\nBuild in this order:\n1. App shell and design system.\n2. Media catalog and search.\n3. Authentication and room database.\n4. Couple mode.\n5. Group mode.\n6. Chat and presence.\n7. Playback adapter and synchronization.\n8. Responsive polish.\n9. Error handling and testing.\n\nAcceptance tests:\n- A user can browse demo movie data.\n- A user can create a couple room.\n- A second user can join using the invite link.\n- A user can create a group room.\n- Participants can send messages and see new messages in real time.\n- Room access restrictions are enforced.\n- Watchlist changes persist.\n- Supported players synchronize correctly.\n- Unsupported players show an honest limitation.\n- The website works on mobile and desktop.\n- No major navigation button is inert.\n- The project builds without TypeScript errors.\n\nAt completion, provide:\n- The implemented application.\n- Project folder structure.\n- Required environment variables in .env.example.\n- Database schema and migrations.\n- Setup and run instructions.\n- Deployment instructions for Vercel and the selected backend.\n- A checklist of working features and integrations that still require credentials or provider approval.\n\nStart building the actual application now. Prioritize functional features, robust architecture, and a polished cinematic user experience over placeholder screens."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Enable Cloud SQL in region asia-south1"
    }
  }
}
